[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports describe three
problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first; the other two keep coming in after it ships.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket, with the reports it covers.
</Callout>

## What the reports say

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads dates month first, and every tenant on file writes the
  day first. Day and month come out swapped, on every channel.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants on booking-v3
  type the month first, as told, and the form reads it day first. Only through the form, only on
  booking-v3. A parser fix does not reach it.
- **`one-day`**: the booking moves by one day, or arrives a day early. A month-first parser swaps
  day and month; it never moves a date by one day. The desk read these right
  (`desk-notes.json`), then filed them under `parser` by tenant. Look at time zones and DST.

## How these were counted

- One report per id. The ticket export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant (its locale and its booking form, `locales.ts`), not from the
  intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser and disagrees on dozens of reports.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move counts under its own problem, not under its tenant's cause.

## The same reports, the way #4471 counts them

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why it happened

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and nobody reads the
body. Reports with many different subjects ended up in one ticket because all of them say "wrong day". The
subject tells you a date is wrong; the tenant, the channel and the body tell you why.

